|> Exercise
    work=ing
    handle=_reorder_derivatives_
    If the functions below denote the
    position, velocity, and acceleration
    of an object, which would be which?
    And if they denote the velocity,
    acceleration, and jerk of an 
    object, which would be which?

    !! Among the functions below, which 
    !! could 
    !! is the
    !! zeroth, first, and second derivative?
    !! (I.e., which is $f$, $f'$, and $f''$,
    !! assuming that relationship exists.)

    |> Image
        src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order.svg

    |> Solution

        The answer is “third graph, first graph, second graph” 
        for either question, because
        the functions form a triple $f$/$f'$/$f''$ in
        that order, as hereby visually verified:

        !! The first graph appears to be the derivative
        !! of the third graph, and the second graph
        !! appears to be the derivative of the first graph,
        !! thus seemingly forming a triple $f$/$f'$/$f''$ in the third/first/second
        !! order:

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_first_pair.svg

        |> Image
            src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_second_pair.svg

        Moreover, velocity is the derivative of position,
        acceleration is the derivative of velocity, and
        jerk is the derivative of acceleration,
        making a triple of the form “$f$/$f'$/$f''$” a match
        for either “position, velocity, accleration”
        or “velocity”, “acceleration”, “jerk” in that order.

        !! With no other ordering of the graphs statisfying a
        !! similar relationship the answer is
        !! therefore “third/second/first”, since either version
        !! of the question is asking for an ordering in which
        !! each subsequent graph is the rate of change of the
        !! previous.

        !! (Hum.)

        !! The only ordering that makes sense is

        !! $$
        !! \begin{gathered}
        !! 1.\,\, \te{third graph}\\
        !! 2.\,\, \te{first graph}\up{1.3} \\
        !! 3.\,\, \te{second graph}\up{1.3}
        !! \end{gathered}
        !! $$

        !! in the order position/velocity/acceleration 
        !! or velocity/acceleration/jerk, because
        !! that is the only order for which each next
        !! graph seems to compute the rate of change of
        !! the previous.
        !! In more detail...
        !! !! For that order, it is easy to inspect that
        !! !! slope of each graph is the value of the next
        !! !! (or is not visibly _not so_, at least):
        !! !! as one can see by inspecting the
        !! !! slope of each graph, and comparing to the value
        !! !! of the next graph, in this order:

        !! !! |> Image
        !! !!     style=margin-bottom:-0.5em
        !! !!     src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_answer.svg

        !! !! |> StarDivider

        !! !! (Ok, in full full detail...

        !! |> Image
        !!     src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_first_pair.svg

        !! |> Image
        !!     src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_second_pair.svg


        !! ...we have these slope-value relationships,
        !! implying...


        !! |> Image
        !!     src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_answer.svg
        
        !! ...this $f$/$f'$/$f''$ relationship, that can 
        !! match onto 
        



        !! !! ...matching slopes against values.

        !! !! The two questions are the same,
        !! !! asking which function is the rate of
        !! !! change of which other. Inspection reveals
        !! !! that the second is the derivative of the
        !! !! first, whic is the derivative of the third:

        !! !! |> Image
        !! !!     src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_answer.svg

        !! !! (Matching slopes against values is the way
        !! !! to convince oneself, if in doubt:)

        !! !! |> Image
        !! !!     src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_first_pair.svg

        !! !! |> Image
        !! !!     src=images/svg_ch4_ex_position_by_time_find_the_order_second_pair.svg

        !! !! The ordering is therefore

        !! !! $$
        !! !! \begin{gathered}
        !! !! 1. \te{third $(f)$}\\
        !! !! 2. \te{first ($f'$)}\up{1.3} \\
        !! !! 3. \te{second ($f''$)}\up{1.3}
        !! !! \end{gathered}
        !! !! $$

        !! !! in either case.

        !! !! As no other ordering of the three
        !! !! functions works similarly, the correspondence is therefore

        !! !! $$
        !! !! \begin{gathered}
        !! !! \te{position} = \te{third $(f)$}\\
        !! !! \te{velocity} = \te{first ($f'$)}\up{1.3} \\
        !! !! \te{acceleration} = \te{second ($f''$)}\up{1.3}
        !! !! \end{gathered}
        !! !! $$

        !! !! or

        !! !! $$
        !! !! \begin{gathered}
        !! !! \te{velocity} = \te{third $(f)$}\\
        !! !! \te{acceleration} = \te{first ($f'$)}\up{1.3} \\
        !! !! \te{jerk} = \te{second ($f''$)}\up{1.3}
        !! !! \end{gathered}
        !! !! $$

        !! !! in either case.
        !! !! !! (in the same order) for the second version of the question.
        !! !! !! in the same order
        !! !! !! depending on which version of the question.
        !! !! !! for the second question.


        !! !! !! _|position = third|_
        !! !! !! _|velocity = first|_
        !! !! !! _|acceleration = third|_

        !! !! !! Therefore the third (“$f$”), first
        !! !! !! (“$f'$”) and second (“$f''$”) functions
        !! !! !! could respectively be the position/velocity/acceleration
        !! !! !! of an object (assuming units were added), 
        !! !! !! or else the velocity/acceleration/jerk of an object
        !! !! !! (assuming units were added).

